node_modules/

# Loop runtime state — self-report and generated inventories, never committed.
#
# `loop/verdicts/*` covers `history/` too, which is where every verdict is archived under its
# iteration number so a rework cannot overwrite the rejection that explained it. Bulky and only ever
# read by a human building a judge fixture out of it.
#
# NOT ignored, and deliberately: `loop/runs/` and `loop/evals/`. Those are the committed record of
# what a run cost and of whether the judge still catches anything — the whole point of them is to be
# diffed against the last one.
loop/JOURNAL.md
loop/STEPS.md
loop/verdicts/*
!loop/verdicts/.gitkeep

# The runner writes the tracker to a temp file and renames it into place. It cleans up after a failed
# rename itself; this is for the case no `catch` can reach — the process dying between the two. Left
# behind and untracked, it makes the NEXT run refuse to start with "working tree is dirty", pointing
# at a file the operator never created.
loop/trackers/*.tmp

# .NET build output
framework/**/bin/
framework/**/obj/
framework/**/TestResults/

# Toolchain output that lands OUTSIDE bin/obj, and so inside the directories the runner watches for
# work an agent failed to commit. `packages.lock.json` appears beside the csproj when a machine-level
# Directory.Build.props sets RestorePackagesWithLockFile; Reqnroll's *.feature.cs lands beside the
# feature files rather than in obj/ under one configuration — measured, that one is inside the fenced
# Features/ directory. Generated code should not be committed in any case.
framework/**/packages.lock.json
framework/**/*.feature.cs

# Visual Studio per-user state: window layout, a SQLite index, a file-content index, test logs.
# It changes every time the IDE is opened or a file is clicked, and .vsidx files are locked tightly
# enough that `git add` fails on them outright.
#
# Bare `.vs/` matches the directory at ANY depth. The first version of this rule was
# `framework/**/.vs/`, which covered the copy the loop trips over and missed the one at the
# repository root — so a `git add -A` committed four files of IDE state before anyone noticed.
.vs/
